00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.040
and as I mentioned before, a widely popular&nbsp;
interview, and very tactically dense interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.200
with Morgan at tim.blog/morganhousel. And&nbsp;
without further ado, morganhousel.com and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.640
Morgan himself, nice to see you again.
Morgan Housel: Nice to see you, Tim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.280
Happy to be back. Thanks for having me.
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely. So let's start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.720
with a cryptic prompt, and I'll let&nbsp;
you run with it, which is Buffett's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.040
Snickers. Where should we go with that?
Morgan Housel: So after Psychology of Money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:45.040
I was really debating what to write next for&nbsp;
my next book. I had thrown around all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.040
different topics and I had some good ideas,&nbsp;
but it really hadn't hit me what I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.440
to write about. And I was at a retreat with&nbsp;
a group of investors about three years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.280
and one of the investors, he was actually a CEO&nbsp;
of a large public company, and he's pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.960
friends with Warren Buffett. And he told this&nbsp;
story that I had never heard before about Buffett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.440
and it's pretty rare to have a story about Buffett&nbsp;
that I've never heard that no one's — everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:15.360
knows every Buffett story, but this was a new one.
And he said, the CEO said, that in 2009 during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.360
peak of the Great Recession when the economy was&nbsp;
in pieces, he was driving around Omaha with Warren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.160
Buffett. Buffett was driving, he was a passenger,&nbsp;
and the CEO said to Warren, and they're driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.640
past closed up shops during the Great Recession,&nbsp;
the whole country's in tatters. And the CEO said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.720
"Warren, how are we ever going to pull out of&nbsp;
this? The country's never going to be the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.480
after this." And Warren said, "Let's call this&nbsp;
guy Jim." That's not his real name, but he said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.640
"Jim, do you know what the bestselling&nbsp;
candy bar was in 1962?" And Jim said, "No."&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.880
And Warren said, "Snickers." And Warren said, "Do&nbsp;
you know what the bestselling candy bar is today?"&nbsp;

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.360
And Jim said, "No."
And Warren said, "Snickers."&nbsp;

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:57.680
And that was the end of the conversation. He just&nbsp;
stopped right there. And that was his answer to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.680
"Is the country ever going to be the same?" And&nbsp;
I think if you look at Warren Buffett's success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.720
he has been betting on things that stay the&nbsp;
same forever. And it really hit me in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.640
moment that this was the book that I wanted to&nbsp;
write, because for my entire career as a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.520
I had been, "cynical" is probably the right&nbsp;
word, and just astounded at how bad people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.520
are at forecasting, not just in finance,&nbsp;
and the stock market, and the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.600
but in politics. And it's been like that forever.
The most famous expert's ability to predict what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.520
going to happen next is atrocious, and&nbsp;
it's always been atrocious. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.800
that just made me cynical. It was just like&nbsp;
the whole pundit industry is a scam. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.560
my view. And then it was like, "Okay, is there a&nbsp;
positive spin to this? How can I use knowing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.240
bad we are at forecasting to try to do better&nbsp;
at my ability to look forward in the future?"&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.400
And I think what I settled on is, people are&nbsp;
so bad at predicting the future because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.720
always trying to predict what's going to change.&nbsp;
They're always trying to predict what's the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.080
new technology? Who's going to win the next&nbsp;
election? When's the next recession going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.240
come? And our ability to do that is zero. It's&nbsp;
virtually zero. But for the people like Buffett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.440
who focus on what's not going to change,&nbsp;
their ability to understand the future is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.840
actually pretty good. And so they're focusing on&nbsp;
parts of human behavior that have never changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.040
that were true 500 years ago, that will be&nbsp;
true 500 years from now. And so I have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.480
idea when the next recession's going to come, but&nbsp;
I know exactly how people are going to respond to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:30.560
greed and fear, because that's never changed.
There's a great quote from Naval where he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.200
"If your life played out a thousand times, a&nbsp;
thousand different iterations of your life, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.480
would be true in 999 of them?" And those are the&nbsp;
things that you want to focus on in life because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.881
those are not things that are guided by luck, or&nbsp;
chance, or just the quirky ways of the world. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:48.440
so that was, that to me was like, "Ah, I love&nbsp;
that framing." And it also occurred to me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.760
a lot of what I had been writing about for the&nbsp;
previous 15 years were things that never change.&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.480
I think in many ways, Psychology of Money is the&nbsp;
psychology of you, the individual. It's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.120
"What's going through your head?" And Same as Ever&nbsp;
is the psychology of us as a collective. It's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:09.000
what do we keep doing as a society, as a group&nbsp;
over, and over, and over again? One other thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.400
that really struck me here that was influential&nbsp;
to this book for me was one of my favorite books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.000
is a book called The Great Depression: A Diary,&nbsp;
and it's written by a lawyer named Benjamin Roth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.120
who, during the Great Depression, kept a&nbsp;
very detailed diary about what he saw all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:28.800
over his town in Ohio that his son published.&nbsp;
And it's the best economics book ever written.&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:35.240
And during that diary, there's one diary&nbsp;
entry from, I think, 1932 where he says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:41.120
"What I see around the Great Depression in&nbsp;
1932, it reminds me exactly of what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:48.240
in 1920 and in 1878 and in 1865." And he's like,&nbsp;
"It's the same forces happening over, and over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.360
and over again." I thought, "Oh, that's really&nbsp;
interesting." And then a couple pages later it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:59.840
dawned on me that what he was writing in&nbsp;
1932 is exactly what happened in 2008.&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.640
The way that people were dealing with uncertainty&nbsp;
and greed and fear. It never changes. It's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:09.000
same movie over, and over, and over again. And so&nbsp;
if you focus on those things that never change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.840
I think it's our only ability to really understand&nbsp;
the future, your future, society's future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.600
the economy's future. But it's just not as&nbsp;
exciting as focusing on what is going to change.&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.320
all of the attention in the media focuses&nbsp;
on what's the next big technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.905
what is going to change.
Tim Ferriss: Hence, the "news."&nbsp;

00:06:24.905 --> 00:06:27.000
Morgan Housel: And so the premise&nbsp;
of this book is just like —&nbsp;

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Exactly, because that's exciting and it's fun. So&nbsp;
the premise of this book is like, let's stop doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.200
that. Let's focus on what we know with certainty&nbsp;
is going to be a part of your future, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.720
the best that we can do to see the future.
Tim Ferriss: I've been excited to have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:48.160
conversation for a couple of reasons and I'll&nbsp;
edge into those by way of maybe a few anecdotes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.440
So one, I'm not going to get the attribution&nbsp;
right and I'm not going to get the phrasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:58.640
totally on point. But there was an announcement,&nbsp;
I think it might've been Donald Knuth, K-N-U-T-H,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.040
but there's a blog post that I wrote some&nbsp;
time ago, which was ["Finding the One Decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.760
That Removes 100 Decisions."] And he made an&nbsp;
announcement when he stopped using email. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

