it in cuneiform, which I never knew was cuneiform&nbsp;
instead of a different pronunciation. In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:02.360
the stories, as you listen to them in these&nbsp;
various podcasts, if you did a search and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.480
replace with a handful of names, this is true&nbsp;
for Letters from a Stoic or the Moral Letters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:13.040
Lucilius as well from Seneca the Younger, if you&nbsp;
just did a search replace with some names, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:20.760
the same set of dynamics, it's the same foibles of&nbsp;
human nature. It's the same consequences of fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:27.640
greed, wrath, fill in the blank.
And my question for you is, given how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:33.000
much you've thought about writing, given how much&nbsp;
you have written, you've written, I would imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.560
hundreds, thousands of pieces and we spoke quite&nbsp;
a bit about writing in our last conversation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:44.160
the fact that writing is on some level, or good&nbsp;
writing, at least the type of writing that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.440
do is intrinsically selfish. We're writing for&nbsp;
at least an audience of one and that I'm just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.160
going to do a quick recap on some of the things&nbsp;
that stuck with me from our last conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.080
which have proven to be true again, and again,&nbsp;
and again, for instance, and please fact check me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:04.920
but good writing is easy, bad writing is hard.
If you're just struggling through, word by word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:09.400
the slog of a piece, chances are it's just&nbsp;
not going to be good. And also if you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.360
writer's block, there's a damn good chance&nbsp;
it's just a shitty idea or a mediocre idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.800
that you're trying to force into some&nbsp;
existence. Of all the things that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:25.945
could write about. How did you choose to do this,&nbsp;
especially after the success of your last book?&nbsp;

00:09:25.945 --> 00:09:29.640
Morgan Housel: I was told many years ago by Jason&nbsp;
Zweig, a great friend and mentor of mine from The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:35.840
Wall Street Journal that you should only write a&nbsp;
book if in your brain you have to do it. Not like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.320
"Oh, I should do it." Or, "Oh, that's a&nbsp;
lot of money that I could get for doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:43.600
it." Only doing it if you're like, "I can't&nbsp;
sleep until I get these ideas on paper."&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.920
And even in blog form, for my entire career,&nbsp;
that's always been the case. I'm not a sit down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.760
at my desk at nine o'clock and publish by four&nbsp;
o'clock kind of person. I've always been, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.400
just going to toss around a bunch of ideas in my&nbsp;
head and I'm only going to sit down and write when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.320
I can't do anything else until I get this done.&nbsp;
The idea is so fresh, and it's just spilling out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:08.800
of me. And there's been plenty of times when we'll&nbsp;
be grocery shopping with my wife and kids and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.200
basically just like, "I have to go. I need to&nbsp;
go sit down at the computer and write this out."&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.160
And the opposite of that, if I'm like, "Oh,&nbsp;
I should probably write something this week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.160
let's force out the words," it's garbage&nbsp;
every single time. And so I feel like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:24.160
didn't necessarily choose to write this book. I&nbsp;
think I would've been really happy just stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:27.240
at Psychology of Money and saying, "That's&nbsp;
great. I'm just going to quit while I'm ahead,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.160
which is a general idea that I really admire.
But I think it got to the point where I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:36.520
"I really — I have to do this." And it struck&nbsp;
me around. It was the early days of COVID when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.720
this really struck me. I was writing almost every&nbsp;
week, almost every day, about things that never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:46.640
change and putting a lot of thought into it.&nbsp;
And it really became a philosophy that guides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:52.720
my entire life. And I think, to tell you the&nbsp;
truth, it relieved a lot of anxiety from my life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.120
once I internalized a lot of these ideas, anxiety&nbsp;
about the future, what's my future hold? What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:01.400
the economy going to do next? During the early&nbsp;
days of COVID, what's COVID going to do next?&nbsp;

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:06.440
Once you admit that we have no idea, we have no&nbsp;
clue, we have no clue what's going to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.040
but that doesn't mean that you're giving up just&nbsp;
becoming a fatalist. It's just like let's shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:16.080
our attention to what we know is going to be true.&nbsp;
And I honestly think I became happier and calmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:23.800
and my life improved once I got to that point.
Tim Ferriss: So in a sense, is that a Serenity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:32.240
Prayer applied to your life through the&nbsp;
lens of human nature over time? In a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:37.000
separating the things that you can change from the&nbsp;
things that you cannot, the things that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:42.585
predict with some certainty versus the many things&nbsp;
that you cannot. Is that what reduced the anxiety?&nbsp;

00:11:42.585 --> 00:11:45.840
Morgan Housel: I think so. And of course this&nbsp;
is not an idea I came up with. That's the core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:50.800
of Stoicism. It's been around for 2,000 years.&nbsp;
It's what can you control and what can you not?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.560
You really need to be able to figure out how to&nbsp;
separate those. So I'm not claiming at all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.800
I came up with these ideas, but once it became —
Tim Ferriss: Wait a second, you created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.947
Buddhism and Stoicism?
Morgan Housel: Exactly.&nbsp;

00:12:00.947 --> 00:12:03.160
Tim Ferriss: Good for you.
Morgan Housel: In 2020. It was amazing. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:09.240
a very productive year. But once I crystallized&nbsp;
them and I could contextualize that in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:14.867
made a lot of sense to me, I honestly think&nbsp;
it improved my life in a pretty profound way.&nbsp;

00:12:14.867 --> 00:12:16.520
Tim Ferriss: So we're going to come back&nbsp;
to that and we're going to come back to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:23.160
quickly. But since I am getting back into writing&nbsp;
and I've been experimenting with fiction largely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:33.480
but I'd like to actually write some nonfiction&nbsp;
blog posts in the old style back when there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:38.960
blog rolls. I want to really rewind the clock&nbsp;
