write a perfect first draft, it's because&nbsp;
I'm not going to leave this sentence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:04.000
I'm writing until it's perfect. I'm not going&nbsp;
to move on to the next sentence until every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.600
word is perfect. That's always how I've been.
Tim Ferriss: I believe Kurt Vonnegut talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:14.960
about swoopers and [bashers]. I may be getting the&nbsp;
wording wrong, but swoopers are the vomit of first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:23.640
draft refine, refined into a diamond, and then&nbsp;
the plodders are the line at a time, one small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:28.680
facet at a time, and then they're done. I tend to&nbsp;
err on the side of being closer to that myself.&nbsp;

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:36.120
However, that doesn't seem to work&nbsp;
unless you have a clear idea of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.280
your structure is going to look like, or&nbsp;
at least of where you're going to start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:44.520
so that you're not then endlessly swapping things&nbsp;
around or scrapping the first half, in which case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:50.120
you're edging more towards that refine, refine,&nbsp;
refine. So how do you figure out the structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:55.920
of a piece or what your lead is going to be?
Morgan Housel: When I start an article, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:00.440
a chapter even, I really have no idea where it's&nbsp;
going to go. It's not like all the ideas are in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:04.840
head and I just need to put them out on paper. And&nbsp;
I feel like the process of writing is the research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:11.080
process for me. And so that's where the plotter&nbsp;
style comes in, which is I write one sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:15.760
and I sit there, and stare at it and it's like,&nbsp;
"Oh, that reminds me of this. Oh, that prompts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.200
another thought that I had. That paragraph I just&nbsp;
wrote reminds me of something else I can pull in."&nbsp;

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:25.560
That's really where it comes from. So coming up&nbsp;
with an idea, honestly, I think most blog posts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:30.840
I start with a headline. And I'm like, "I want to&nbsp;
write an article called Everything is Cyclical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:35.360
Whatever it might be. And let's just run with&nbsp;
that. That idea, because everything is cyclical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:39.000
and I'm sure I could put together a story about&nbsp;
why that is and a couple examples of that. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:44.160
let's just start there and see what happens."&nbsp;
And I just start throwing things on the page.&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:50.520
And usually within the process of a blog post or&nbsp;
a book chapter, I'll go for three walks around my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:54.600
neighborhood. And during that walk I'm a hundred&nbsp;
percent focused on what I'm– writing and thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:59.480
about, "What did I just write? Is that true? Oh,&nbsp;
actually that reminds me of something else. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:04.000
that's always a process." If I'm sitting at my&nbsp;
desk, I really can't get my brain to work as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.200
well as it is when I'm getting up and walking&nbsp;
around. So very often, too, if the weather's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.000
bad outside in the middle of walking, or in&nbsp;
the middle of writing, I'll get up and fold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:15.160
the laundry or get up and do the dishes, get up&nbsp;
and just walk around the house. It's just like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:19.640
I think movement is really critical to forming&nbsp;
new thoughts and moving the piece along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:23.920
which is so critical when you don't know where&nbsp;
it's going to go when you started writing it.&nbsp;

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:32.600
Tim Ferriss: So let's look at a post of yours. And&nbsp;
I read three in prepping for this conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.040
"Respect and Admiration," "Rich and&nbsp;
Anonymous," and then "Expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:43.680
Debt." How did you decide to write "Rich&nbsp;
and Anonymous?" And maybe that's a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:50.200
move into discussing some of the content,&nbsp;
some of the messages, some of the stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:55.040
in that piece. But how did that come to be?
Morgan Housel: "Rich and Anonymous" came to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:59.920
because — this was probably about two years ago,&nbsp;
I did a consulting session with a group of NBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:05.600
athletes. Most of them were NBA rookies. And the&nbsp;
topic of conversation was everybody knows that NBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:10.920
athletes, all pro athletes have a very high rate&nbsp;
of going bankrupt. They have a three-to-five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:14.080
career where they make tens of millions of&nbsp;
dollars, they blow it all, and then they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:18.640
done. Everyone knows the story. So the topic of&nbsp;
conversation was like, how do we prevent that?&nbsp;

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:25.400
And one of the athletes, who's 19, from the NBA,&nbsp;
he had very recently been drafted to deca-million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:29.640
dollar contract, massive amount of money. He&nbsp;
brought up this fact that I thought was so astute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:35.080
particularly for a 19-year-old. And he said, "When&nbsp;
you grow up in inner city poverty and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:41.160
make tens of millions of dollars at age 19, that's&nbsp;
not your money. That is Mom's money, Dad's money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.920
cousin's money, brother's money, neighbor's money,&nbsp;
Grandma's money. You can't just tell all of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:51.160
'I got my money, good luck to you all' back in&nbsp;
inner city poverty. It doesn't work like that.'"&nbsp;

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:55.680
And he said the biggest reason that athletes go&nbsp;
bankrupt is not because they bought themselves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.960
mansion, it's because they bought their fifth&nbsp;
cousin a modest house and they felt so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:04.440
obligation to help them. So that was this idea&nbsp;
that I came up, I don't know if I came up with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:11.240
but I called it "social debt." And I think with&nbsp;
every dollar that you earn comes some amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:15.400
social debt. And for somebody like an athlete&nbsp;
or a movie star, there's a ton of social — for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:19.800
every dollar that they earn, there's probably&nbsp;
$4 of social debt that comes on top of it.&nbsp;

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:23.960
And it's very easy to track your net worth,&nbsp;
it's incredibly difficult to track your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:28.080
social debt. It's this obscure figure, but&nbsp;
it's always there. And even if we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:33.080
talking about deca-million dollar athletes, I&nbsp;
think for the average person who gets a raise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:39.800
their salary goes from $50,000 to $55,000. With&nbsp;
that even comes an increase in expectations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:43.520
now they expect to live in a bigger apartment&nbsp;
to wear nicer clothes, to eat out more. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:48.880
just their expectation increase that comes with&nbsp;
that is a form of social debt that came with it.&nbsp;

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:53.040
And so that is this idea. I'm pretty sure&nbsp;
that Naval came up with this phrasing, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:59.080
that the best spot you want to be in life is rich&nbsp;
and anonymous. The worst is poor and famous, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:03.600
was maybe Monica Lewinsky or something. Like very&nbsp;
famous and no money, that's the worst position you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:09.280
can be in. Rich and anonymous is the best. I spent&nbsp;
some time with a family two or three years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:15.960
The family is worth $8 billion and if you Google&nbsp;
their name, nothing comes up. They are completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:20.600
anonymous. They're not on any Forbes list, they've&nbsp;
not done any interviews, they donate their money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:25.800
anonymously, and that's all very intentional.
And so this family is worth $8 billion and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:30.320
have virtually no social debt. They can just&nbsp;
walk down the street, they can go to any cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:35.640
restaurant. Nobody knows who they are, and they've&nbsp;
been very cognizant of doing that. And when I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:41.240
how happy they are and how well-balanced they are,&nbsp;
how well-balanced their children were, it was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:46.480
that's the spot that everyone wants to be in.
So even if you're not worth $8 billion like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:51.280
realizing that every dollar of net worth that&nbsp;
you gain comes with probably at least a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:56.947
pennies of social debt, I think is a really&nbsp;
important thing to think about and contemplate.&nbsp;

00:19:56.947 --> 00:20:00.320
Tim Ferriss: So let me ask&nbsp;
you more about this family.&nbsp;

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:05.400
All right, eight billion, that's non-trivial.&nbsp;
So you would think that this family would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:11.000
on many radars, many lists, whether they&nbsp;
want to appear there or not. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:15.320
it's probably sector dependent. I don't want&nbsp;
you to share anything you don't want to share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:21.800
but there may be some sectors, there are more&nbsp;
sectors, that are prone to being publicly exposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:27.400
or of public interest than others. If you're a&nbsp;
defense contractor, maybe the people who read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:33.040
People magazine are not as interested as if&nbsp;
you are a movie star, or fill in the blank.&nbsp;

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:43.000
But what are some of the decisions, outside&nbsp;
of anonymous donations, that you think were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:49.800
critical for them, or might have been part of&nbsp;
this plan? And then I have some other questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:54.280
related to their kids and the happiness of&nbsp;
their kids. Because last time we spoke about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.280 --> 00:21:00.760
I'm blanking on the name of the book, but I think&nbsp;
it was the Vanderbilt fortune and how almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:07.680
uniformly miserable everyone was downstream of the&nbsp;
inflation adjusted whatever crazy number it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:15.120
400 billion dollars or something like that.
So coming back to the question that I led with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:19.760
what were some of the decisions&nbsp;
or types of decisions they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:22.560
that contributed to that outcome?
Morgan Housel: What's so interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:25.800
about this family, and of course I'm not going&nbsp;
to give any details to disclose who they were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:29.920
but they made their money in a very ethical&nbsp;
way. It's not like they're trying to hide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:33.600
fact of how they made their money. And what's also&nbsp;
interesting is that if I told you the name of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:37.680
company, you would know it. It's not this hidden&nbsp;
obscure business. You would know what it is.&nbsp;

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:42.040
But again, they've been very intentional&nbsp;
about doing everything anonymously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:47.680
not being part of any fundraiser gala, not —&nbsp;
living, actually. They actually live a fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:50.265
good life, but it was all based around —
Tim Ferriss: I would hope so.&nbsp;

00:21:50.265 --> 00:21:53.720
Morgan Housel: But it was based around this idea&nbsp;
of, "How do we not ruin our children?" I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:58.400
that was really it. How do we raise children who&nbsp;
are well-balanced and understand the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:03.480
are not treated differently than anyone else?
Back to the Vanderbilts, which is such a crazy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:09.880
story, there is this anecdote in one of the books&nbsp;
where one of the Vanderbilt grandchildren wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:14.720
learn how to play soccer. So they gather up all&nbsp;
the servants in the mansion that they live in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:18.480
and they're like, "Go play soccer with&nbsp;
our kid." And they're watching it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.080
the servants who are adults —
Tim Ferriss: I have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:23.280
imagine where this is going.
Morgan Housel: The servants are all just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:27.360
getting their ass kicked by this eight-year-old.&nbsp;
They let every goal go through. No one is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.440
to try to take the ball from him.
Tim Ferriss: I knew it.&nbsp;

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:33.600
Morgan Housel: And of course the eight-year-old&nbsp;
is like, "I'm so good! I'm so good at soccer!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:37.440
So as soon as people know who you are, and&nbsp;
what your last name is, how much — they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:42.404
treat you differently. And that's not —
Tim Ferriss: And laugh at all your dumb jokes.&nbsp;

00:22:42.404 --> 00:22:43.360
Morgan Housel: Is a terrible thing. Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: [inaudible 00:28:17] all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.280
your dumb ideas.
Morgan Housel: I don't think I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:49.880
ever talked about this before, but I've told my&nbsp;
wife this. In my tenure at the Collaborative Fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:53.480
there was a period early on when I was doing&nbsp;
deals and I was meeting with entrepreneurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:58.000
entrepreneurs who were trying to raise money from&nbsp;
the Collaborative Fund. And it was astounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.400
that when I was talking to an entrepreneur who&nbsp;
wanted to raise money, every joke that I said was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:08.160
so funny. Every insight that I had was so wise.
When people want something from you, they treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:13.000
you differently, and it's not good. So this —&nbsp;
back to the family that I did some work with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:18.840
they wanted to move mountains to avoid that. They&nbsp;
wanted their children to be treated normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:23.640
and I have so much respect and admiration for&nbsp;
that. And going into this, a lot of what I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:29.520
going to do was talk to the children about money.&nbsp;
That was my role in this experience. And if I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:34.520
honest, going into it, I expected them all to be&nbsp;
spoiled little brats. And they were the opposite.&nbsp;

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:39.600
They were the most well-balanced, polite,&nbsp;
well-mannered kids. And what they had that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:45.800
I thought was so great is a sense of anxiety&nbsp;
about who they are in the world. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:49.480
really rare for a child who grows up&nbsp;
with that kind of means. Most of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:54.160
it's in their identity that "I'm special, I'm&nbsp;
the best, I'm the richest, I'm the smartest."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:58.400
That's tends to be their identity. And these were&nbsp;
completely normal. If you didn't know their net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:02.187
worth and you met them, you would think that they&nbsp;
were the children of plumbers and accountants.&nbsp;

00:24:02.187 --> 00:24:05.280
Tim Ferriss: If I could pause for one second,&nbsp;
and I want to get into some specifics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:08.920
maybe what you took away from that,&nbsp;
or some of the things that they did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:15.800
if you're able to share to produce that outcome.
Now, maybe just out of the box, this family is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:21.400
programmed a little differently. So we're looking&nbsp;
at maybe, I don't want to say reversed causality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:27.960
but we might be looking at something&nbsp;
that existed out of the box, so to speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:32.120
psychologically. However, there are probably&nbsp;
some levers that people can pull. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:36.080
doesn't just apply to families with $8 billion.
For instance, I grew up without very much money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:43.480
and in my neighborhood on Long Island, the family&nbsp;
that made 75k versus the families that made 40k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:51.800
had a similar psychological dynamic. The kids&nbsp;
had better toys, they had bigger TV, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.800 --> 00:25:00.600
came with it, unchecked, a certain arrogance,&nbsp;
even though they were by no stretch close to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:10.800
family with $8 billion. The positional economics&nbsp;
of it were something similar. And as I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:18.000
more about kids myself, I think about this a lot&nbsp;
because you don't need to be in the stratosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:24.040
economically for this to be a relevant topic, to&nbsp;
think about. And if you're born in the US with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:30.080
your native language as English in a reasonably&nbsp;
safe place, you've already won a lot of lotteries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:35.960
right? So from a global perspective, you can&nbsp;
also subconsciously develop some pretty kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.960
of perverse orientations throughout the world. So&nbsp;
what are some things that they did or that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:48.960
can do to produce kids or bend the arc a little&nbsp;
bit towards kids who are less entitled and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:52.320
self-aware in the way that you're describing?
Morgan Housel: I've thought about this a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:54.619
and it's a question I get a lot.
Tim Ferriss: I'm sure.&nbsp;

00:25:54.619 --> 00:25:57.120
Morgan Housel: About how do you raise&nbsp;
kids that don't grow up to be spoiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:01.960
little brats? And I've come to the conclusion&nbsp;
that the only answer is give them less money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:05.400
That's it. That's the only thing. It's&nbsp;
the answer that nobody wants to hear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:09.720
but I don't think there's any other way around&nbsp;
it. The reason that money is valuable is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:13.920
you usually have to work hard for it. If you don't&nbsp;
have to work hard for it becomes not that valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:16.040
to you. In the sense of you just throw it around.
Tim Ferriss: I see. So give them less money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.840
not just in the sense of inheritance,&nbsp;
but you're saying on an ongoing basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:24.360
like allowance, toys, et cetera.
Morgan Housel: Yes. Totally. And of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:27.960
course like everyone, I just kind of anchored&nbsp;
to my old childhood when thinking about this. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:34.360
parents. Particularly later in my childhood, they&nbsp;
had some means and I got very little from it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:38.040
at the time that sucked. I looked back on it and&nbsp;
think that was great. That was so good because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:43.560
from age 16 I learned how to be self-dependent and&nbsp;
I learned how to work and I learned how to do it.&nbsp;

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:49.000
There's a good friend of mine named Chris Davis&nbsp;
who his grandfather, Shelby Davis, is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:54.120
Mount Rushmore investors. He became a billionaire&nbsp;
in the 1980s just from picking stocks. And Chris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:59.640
Davis and his cousins and siblings got nothing,&nbsp;
did not get one single cent from it. And his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:04.720
grandfather, Shelby Davis, would tell them, "I'm&nbsp;
not going to rob you of the opportunity of earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:10.600
your own income." And that's the right way to&nbsp;
view it. And I think every cent that you give your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:14.360
children, particularly your older children,&nbsp;
you are robbing them of that opportunity.&nbsp;

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:19.320
My wife and I think a lot about that with our own&nbsp;
kids, of every cell in your body as a parent wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:24.920
to say, "I want to provide for my kids, support my&nbsp;
kids, boost my kids, give them all the benefit."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:29.880
And it's so hard to push back against that natural&nbsp;
mentality and say, "No, I'm going to make you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:34.520
it on your own." I'll give you one perfect example&nbsp;
here. I grew up ski racing. My son who's now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:39.760
seven, I started taking him skiing about two years&nbsp;
ago. And because I'm a fairly advanced skier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:43.880
it was no problem for me on the first day to&nbsp;
