hometown, and I was driving along the freeway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.480 --> 00:41:06.120
and there's a giant building, one of the tallest&nbsp;
buildings in town that on top of the building in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:11.720
enormous, probably 50-foot letters says, "Buffett&nbsp;
Cancer Research Center." So he's funded it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.720 --> 00:41:16.587
he's bought it. Warren drives by past that&nbsp;
building every day and sees that. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.587 --> 00:41:20.560
is no way that seeing your name on something&nbsp;
like that, there's no way that he's the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:24.840
Warren Buffett who drove past that building&nbsp;
when he was 13. It's going to completely change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.760
just your view of who you are in the world.
To your point about, let's not leave this on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:34.280
sad note, because you're right, I do think money&nbsp;
can be used to give yourself a better life. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:39.040
distinction we need to make is the distinction&nbsp;
between happiness and contentment. Does money buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:44.240
happiness? I think that answer is pretty firmly&nbsp;
no. Can money buy contentment? I think that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:48.560
pretty firm yes. And that's great, contentment&nbsp;
is a great trait that makes your life better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.560 --> 00:41:51.280
but it's very different from happiness.
Tim Ferriss: Could you just define that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:55.520
term so we understand what we're talking about?
Morgan Housel: I would say happiness is you wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.520 --> 00:41:59.920
up grinning ear to ear. Happiness is you're out&nbsp;
at a bar with your friends. Happiness is you hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:03.480
the funniest joke you've ever heard, and that's&nbsp;
what I think people strive for and they think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:08.080
money's going to give to them and it won't. But I&nbsp;
think contentment is you just wake up with a low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:12.040
or virtually no level of anxiety. You're like, I'm&nbsp;
good. I'm pretty happy — I'm pretty satisfied with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:16.080
my career. I'm satisfied with my relationships.&nbsp;
I'm satisfied with the house that I live in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:22.120
That's not happiness though. I think money can&nbsp;
reduce the number of sad days that you have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:25.480
but it's probably not going to increase the&nbsp;
number of happy days that you have. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:29.580
that's awesome. If you can do that, that's a&nbsp;
huge life improvement, but it's not happiness.&nbsp;

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:34.200
Tim Ferriss: Unless you have standup comedy&nbsp;
budget and you go to more standup comedy shows.&nbsp;

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:37.600
Morgan Housel: See, that's actually a great point.
Tim Ferriss: I'm not really kidding. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.840
something that I do quite a lot of.
Morgan Housel: I've been on such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.840 --> 00:42:46.280
a comedy binge lately, just Netflix&nbsp;
specials. And I've said this many times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:49.427
but I think comedians are the only good thought&nbsp;
leaders because when you listen to good comedy —&nbsp;

00:42:49.427 --> 00:42:52.185
Tim Ferriss: They're the only&nbsp;
practical philosophers left.&nbsp;

00:42:52.185 --> 00:42:56.640
Morgan Housel: Exactly. And not only do you&nbsp;
laugh, but you get smarter. I think George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.640 --> 00:43:01.280
Carlin was a bonafide genius. I think Bill&nbsp;
Burr is a genius. Those guys understand human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:07.000
behavior better than any psychology PhD does.
Tim Ferriss: So let's take a hard left from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:13.440
what we're talking about. Let's talk about&nbsp;
avalanches. I'll let you take the baton and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:18.680
go from there. Avalanches scare the living&nbsp;
hell out of me. I do have a decent amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:24.840
I have no expert skier to the extent that&nbsp;
you used to compete and so on, but I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:28.520
done in the last handful of years, quite a&nbsp;
lot of side country and back country skiing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:37.760
and this just scares the wits out of me, does&nbsp;
not get less scary with each passing year. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:43.400
tell me about your experience with avalanches.
Morgan Housel: So I grew up as a competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:50.000
ski racer in Lake Tahoe, and most of those big&nbsp;
years were when I was a teenager, 16, 17, 18.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:55.160
And my friends and I, there were about a dozen of&nbsp;
us on the Squaw Valley ski team, and we skied six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:55.160 --> 00:44:01.000
days a week, 10 months a year, we skied all over&nbsp;
the world, always traveling around. And one day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:06.160
this is February of 2001, I was 17 years old&nbsp;
at the time, and Lake Tahoe where we lived, had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:12.720
just been dumped on, just got an absolute monster&nbsp;
blanket of snow, dumped on. And the layering of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:19.200
snow was very important to the story. So at one&nbsp;
point, Squaw got about two feet of very light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:25.200
fluffy snow. And then after that it got about&nbsp;
two feet of very wet heavy snow on top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:29.240
which we didn't think about this at the time,&nbsp;
but that creates a textbook perfect avalanche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.240 --> 00:44:33.480
condition because you have heavy snow on&nbsp;
top of light snow, it slides very easily.&nbsp;

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:38.280
So one day in February 2001, myself and two of my&nbsp;
friends, Brendan Allen and Bryan Richmond, were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:42.000
skiing and we would ski out of bounds at Squaw&nbsp;
Valley. We would duck under the ropes that say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:46.920
"Do not cross," to ski the untouched, untracked&nbsp;
parts of the mountain that are so much fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:50.760
do. And when you do that, there's no chairlift&nbsp;
at the bottom. We would ski to the bottom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:55.960
then hitchhike back once we got spit out on a&nbsp;
back country road. The three of us did this run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.960 --> 00:45:00.640
on the backside of Squaw. We had done it maybe a&nbsp;
half dozen times before, not that often because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:05.760
it's such a pain in the ass to hitchhike, but we&nbsp;
skied down. It was so great, it was so awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:11.240
And halfway down we triggered a small avalanche&nbsp;
and it was pretty small. It came up to our knees.&nbsp;

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:17.280
And it's the most bizarre feeling because you&nbsp;
very suddenly have no control at all because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:22.480
rather than pushing on the snow to gain traction,&nbsp;
the snow's pushing you. So all of a sudden you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:28.000
from skiing to I'm on a roller coaster is&nbsp;
how it feels. But it ended pretty quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:31.360
and we got to the bottom. And I remember saying&nbsp;
to Brendan, I remember saying like, "Holy shit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:35.360
did you see that avalanche?" We laughed about it&nbsp;
and we hitchhiked back and didn't say another word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.360 --> 00:45:40.880
about it. We get back down to Squaw and Brendan&nbsp;
and Bryan say, "Hey, let's do it again." And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.880 --> 00:45:45.920
have no idea why, but I said, "I don't want to do&nbsp;
it again." I think hitchhiking kind of freaked me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:50.120
out. But Brendan and Bryan said, "Okay, we're&nbsp;
going to do it again." And I said, "Hey, rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.120 --> 00:45:54.040
than hitchhiking, I'll drive my car around and&nbsp;
pick you guys up so you don't have to hitchhike."&nbsp;

00:45:54.040 --> 00:45:59.120
So we went our separate ways. Brendan and&nbsp;
Bryan went and did the run again. I went down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:02.560
took my boots off, drove around to pick them up.&nbsp;
And when I got to the spot where I was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.560 --> 00:46:07.040
pick them up, they were not there. And I didn't&nbsp;
think that much about it. I thought maybe they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:11.400
had already hitchhiked back. I think I was late.&nbsp;
So I was like, I felt bad that I was late picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:15.080
them up. And they had already left. And I drove&nbsp;
back to our locker room and they weren't there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.080 --> 00:46:21.040
either. And no one had seen them. And this was&nbsp;
before cell phones, but prior to cell phones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:24.760
people were very comfortable being out of touch,&nbsp;
unlike they are today. If you didn't know where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.760 --> 00:46:28.880
someone was, it was like, yeah, whatever. They're&nbsp;
somewhere else. I didn't think anything of it.&nbsp;

00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:35.080
And later that day, Bryan's mom called me and she&nbsp;
said, "Hey, Bryan didn't show up for work today."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:39.480
And she also just spoken to Brendan's dad.&nbsp;
And Brendan missed a dentist appointment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:44.320
afternoon. And she said, "Do you know where they&nbsp;
are?" And I said, "Yeah, we skied the backside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:50.160
Squaw, which we were not supposed to do, was&nbsp;
out bounds, and I was going to pick them up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:54.040
but they never showed up and no one's seen them&nbsp;
since." And Bryan's mom, who's an expert skier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:58.800
said, "Oh my God." And she clicked the phone,&nbsp;
she hung up. And I think in that moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:05.080
both of us figured out what happened without&nbsp;
saying it. Both of us knew what happened. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:08.680
the day went on, we called the police, we got&nbsp;
a missing person's report. The police didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:08.680 --> 00:47:12.920
take it seriously at all. But eventually that&nbsp;
evening we got search and rescue involved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:16.360
they went out, it was still a blizzard.
They had a team of search dogs and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:16.360 --> 00:47:22.480
giant floodlights to search for them. And the&nbsp;
next morning, about 9:00 a.m. the next morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:27.480
the search dogs had honed in on a spot in the&nbsp;
avalanche field and rescuers with these giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.480 --> 00:47:34.240
pro poles to probe the snow found Brendan and&nbsp;
Bryan buried about six feet down from a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.240 --> 00:47:39.240
avalanche. The rescuers had just said it looked&nbsp;
like half the mountain had been torn away. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:46.000
they were dead, of course. And so I was 17. That&nbsp;
had a huge profound impact on my life. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:49.511
not that unique of a story. Everyone has lost&nbsp;
somebody close to them. Everyone's had a near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.511 --> 00:47:55.720
death experience themselves. But this was my&nbsp;
version of that, and it shredded me, completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.720 --> 00:48:02.120
tore me to pieces. And it wasn't until years later&nbsp;
that I had pieced together a couple of takeaways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:06.560
from this that impacted my life personally.
One was my decision to not go on the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:06.560 --> 00:48:11.120
run with them was completely thoughtless and&nbsp;
brainless. I didn't weigh the pros and cons. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.120 --> 00:48:15.480
didn't say, "Oh no, it's too dangerous." I didn't&nbsp;
think anything of it. I think I was probably just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:19.280
tired. And I said, "You guys go do another&nbsp;
run. And I won't." If I had went with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:24.560
100 percent chance I would've died. So the most&nbsp;
important decision that I ever made in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.560 --> 00:48:29.360
it was not where to go to college. It was not whom&nbsp;
to marry. The most important decision I ever made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:29.360 --> 00:48:33.480
in my life was to not go on the second run with&nbsp;
them. And I put no thought into it. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.480 --> 00:48:39.400
complete random fluke. And so one of the takeaways&nbsp;
there for me was just like how fragile life is.&nbsp;

00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:42.560
There's this great quote from Tim Urban, who&nbsp;
I think has probably been on your show before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:42.560 --> 00:48:47.280
where he says, "If you had a time machine and&nbsp;
you can go back in time, you would be terrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:53.080
because you would realize that the tiniest little&nbsp;
know-nothing decision that you make can flourish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.080 --> 00:48:57.640
into completely change your life in good ways." In&nbsp;
good ways and bad. Like most people, how they met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:57.640 --> 00:49:02.000
their spouse was a complete random fluke that&nbsp;
ended up really important to them. And so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:02.000 --> 00:49:07.080
realize this is how incredibly fragile life is and&nbsp;
how there might be a decision that you make today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:12.360
that I make today, that we think nothing about.&nbsp;
It's the random decision that goes on to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:12.360 --> 00:49:18.080
everything. So that story, that experience had&nbsp;
a profound impact on me on thinking about risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:22.720
thinking about the tail end consequences&nbsp;
of risk, thinking about how fragile the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:28.600
world is. It went on to impact virtually&nbsp;
everything in my life from that one day.&nbsp;

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:36.760
Tim Ferriss: I'm so sorry, obviously, for the&nbsp;
loss of your friends. And the story for all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:42.560
reasons you described is terrifying not just&nbsp;
from the avalanche perspective, but from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:51.040
angle of looking at how small decisions can have&nbsp;
huge impacts. And also how I think as we discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:55.320
in the last conversation, risk is what's left&nbsp;
when you've thought of everything else. Now in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:55.320 --> 00:50:01.520
this particular case, I suppose if you'd really&nbsp;
taken a moment to sit down with the pro and cons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.520 --> 00:50:05.880
you probably would've figured out that it was&nbsp;
a bad idea. But in the moment, you could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:09.800
very easily made the wrong decision.
I suppose part of what I'm wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:09.800 --> 00:50:16.440
because you've become very good at interrogating&nbsp;
the world and your own thoughts with questions. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:16.440 --> 00:50:20.920
that particular moment, you made a snap judgment.&nbsp;
It's not really a snap judgment. You made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:27.800
snap decision that saved your life, but you have&nbsp;
questions that you seem to explore or revisit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:33.080
some regularity. Some of them we discussed in the&nbsp;
last interview, but I'm just going to read a few.&nbsp;

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:37.400
So what are we ignoring today that will seem&nbsp;
shockingly obvious in a year? Which of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.400 --> 00:50:43.320
current views would change if our incentives&nbsp;
were different? And it goes on and on. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.320 --> 00:50:47.080
there are many others. I mean, there's one that I&nbsp;
had not heard before, which is what hassle are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:51.840
trying to eliminate that's actually an unavoidable&nbsp;
cost of success? That's pretty interesting to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.840 --> 00:50:56.680
Who has the right answers, but we ignore because&nbsp;
they're not articulate? What looks unsustainable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.680 --> 00:51:04.000
that is actually a new trend we haven't accepted&nbsp;
yet? So my macro question about these questions is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:08.840
how you arrived at these questions, whether it was&nbsp;
coming up with them or borrowing them or adapting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:19.705
them, and which of these might be worth exploring&nbsp;
as our next chapter in this conversation?&nbsp;

00:51:19.705 --> 00:51:23.080
Morgan Housel: I think maybe the core of a lot&nbsp;
of these questions is that everybody, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:29.360
myself, thinks that their experience in life is&nbsp;
roughly how the world works without realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:35.760
that their experience in life is so very unique&nbsp;
to their life. And so you and I are white American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:40.000
males, and I think it's common for us naturally&nbsp;
to think that the experiences that you and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:45.640
had and how people treat us is how everyone else&nbsp;
is treated. And of course, that's not true. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.640 --> 00:51:49.600
so I think when you accept that the world that&nbsp;
I've experienced is so different from the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:49.600 --> 00:51:53.360
that you've experienced and other people, then&nbsp;
I think you just start asking these questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.360 --> 00:51:58.720
about what else is out there that I haven't seen&nbsp;
that I'm oblivious to. And the fact is that I and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:03.440
you and everyone is oblivious to 99.99 percent of&nbsp;
the world, completely oblivious to how it works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:07.480
because we're centered on what we've experienced.
So that's where a lot of it comes. I think a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:13.080
of it too, and this is an unhealthy mindset, but&nbsp;
I spend 99 percent of my life thinking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:17.440
past and contemplating the future. The worst&nbsp;
thing that you can do, like virtually never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:22.600
present. So I think I'm constantly interrogating&nbsp;
the past of what could it have been? Why did that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.600 --> 00:52:26.720
happen? What would my life have been like&nbsp;
if that happened? My parents were visiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:26.720 --> 00:52:33.760
us just last week, and my mother was and is at&nbsp;
a very high risk of breast cancer. By age 42,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:33.760 --> 00:52:37.560
she was the oldest surviving female in her family,&nbsp;
her sister and mother, her grandmother, everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:43.280
had died of breast cancer. And my mother is now 70&nbsp;
and she's healthy. Fingers crossed, knock on wood.&nbsp;

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:48.440
But we were talking the other day about when we&nbsp;
were growing up and myself and my siblings were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.440 --> 00:52:54.000
children, that my father and mother would cry. If&nbsp;
my mother had a lump that needed to get checked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:59.640
out, they would hold each other and cry about&nbsp;
this is going to be the one. And it's so bonkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:04.320
for me to contemplate if my childhood had ended&nbsp;
up differently and it so easily could have, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:08.000
only could it have, it probably should have,&nbsp;
statistically she should have died of breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.000 --> 00:53:11.880
cancer 30 years ago. And the fact that it didn't&nbsp;
— so I actually spent, this is probably unhealthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:16.320
but I spent a lot of my time contemplating what&nbsp;
would life have been like if my mother died when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:21.400
I was 10 years old? I'll take that back. I think&nbsp;
that is a pretty healthy thing to think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:25.680
to imagine a life that's different than it could&nbsp;
have been. Because I think it prepares you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:30.960
a more variant future than you're expecting.
And the truth is that nothing would've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:30.960 --> 00:53:33.840
the same. I would've gone to different schools.&nbsp;
I would've had different friends. I would've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:36.960
had a different mindset. I would've had a&nbsp;
different career if that had happened. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:39.720
you can say that for a billion different&nbsp;
things in life. And then you realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:44.760
the path that got you to where you are today&nbsp;
was completely unforeseeable and completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:49.840
random. And in many ways outside of your control.&nbsp;
Everyone, I think particularly successful people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:54.840
want to think that their success was entirely,&nbsp;
if not overwhelmingly, due to the decisions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.840 --> 00:54:00.040
they made. And I think in virtually any&nbsp;
condition, in any example, that's not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.040 --> 00:54:04.120
case. There were good decisions that you made —
Tim Ferriss: Very fragile framework, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.120 --> 00:54:10.800
Because you're then either excellent or terrible&nbsp;
depending on the outcome. And in both cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:10.800 --> 00:54:17.720
you're not factoring all of the chance&nbsp;
involved. It seems to be a fragile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.720 --> 00:54:22.440
mindset in a sense. It's a vulnerable mindset.
Morgan Housel: There's a German philosopher whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:26.480
name I'm forgetting, but he has this idea,&nbsp;
and this is probably a pretty common idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.480 --> 00:54:30.440
I don't know if he came up with this, but&nbsp;
he just talked about it, that there are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.440 --> 00:54:34.800
infinite number of possible worlds, an infinite&nbsp;
number of ways the world could have turned out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:39.160
and we just happen to live in this one. There are&nbsp;
just an infinite number of alternative histories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:42.400
It could have gone another way. During the&nbsp;
Revolutionary War in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:46.640
it was the Battle of Long Island, and George&nbsp;
Washington and his troops were very close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.640 --> 00:54:49.040
being wiped out by the British. All the British&nbsp;
had to do was — they basically had them cornered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.040 --> 00:54:52.400
just outside of Brooklyn, and all they had&nbsp;
to do was sail up the East River and it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.400 --> 00:54:55.720
all have been over, and that was their plan.
And George Washington's troops were starving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:55.720 --> 00:55:01.280
and hungry and they were whipped. And the night&nbsp;
that the British were planning to sail up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:06.080
river and corner him, the winds changed direction&nbsp;
and turned the other way and they could not sail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:10.480
up the East River. And that gave George Washington&nbsp;
and his troops just enough time to get away. David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:16.080
McCullough, who was one of the world's foremost&nbsp;
authorities on this topic, was interviewed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:20.720
Charlie Rose, and Charlie Rose said, "If the&nbsp;
winds were blowing the other direction that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:25.760
would there have been any United States?" and&nbsp;
David McCullough said, "Absolutely not." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:29.920
entire reason this exists is because the winds&nbsp;
in Brooklyn changed directions last night. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.920 --> 00:55:34.880
you hear enough stories like that, you realize&nbsp;
literally nothing is certain about the future.&nbsp;

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:39.440
It could all go in any different direction for&nbsp;
the tiniest little reason, not a big reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.440 --> 00:55:45.200
but, for me, it was not going on a second&nbsp;
run, and I thought about it afterwards. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:50.520
probably been on something like 3,000 runs with&nbsp;
Brendan and Bryan over the course of my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:55.280
probably something in that neighborhood. And how&nbsp;
many times did I say, "Hey, you guys do another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:58.160
