01:08:03.640 --> 01:08:07.861
if you respect them for their faults&nbsp;
as much as you do their good traits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.861 --> 01:08:09.240
and I think that was really true, that in —
Tim Ferriss: What does that mean though? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:12.760
sounds good. I'm going to push back a little hard,&nbsp;
because I'm not like, "Yeah, this person's always&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:12.760 --> 01:08:16.585
late. I really respect that they're always&nbsp;
fucking late when they show up to dinner."&nbsp;

01:08:16.585 --> 01:08:19.800
Morgan Housel: No, I totally understand what you&nbsp;
mean, and I think maybe respect is the wrong word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:19.800 --> 01:08:21.665
but it's more acceptance.
Tim Ferriss: I see.&nbsp;

01:08:21.665 --> 01:08:25.000
Morgan Housel: If my wife does something that&nbsp;
irritates me, and of course, that's happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:25.000 --> 01:08:30.960
and vice versa, more so vice versa, I have to go&nbsp;
out of my way, but I try to go out of my way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:37.200
say, "I know she means well. I know she's doing&nbsp;
the best she can," even if it really bothered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.200 --> 01:08:42.600
me. And I think, if you can hold onto that, it's&nbsp;
easier not to look over things, but to compromise&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.600 --> 01:08:47.960
on things. Everyone knows the friendship with&nbsp;
your best buddy, not your spouse or your mate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.960 --> 01:08:52.640
but your best buddy, that all it takes is for&nbsp;
one person to pick up the phone and give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.640 --> 01:08:56.560
a call. And if you can do that, you can keep a&nbsp;
friendship going for 20 years, 30 years. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:56.560 --> 01:09:02.880
think, if one person in the relationship really&nbsp;
wants us to work, you can really make it work.&nbsp;

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:07.840
I have a close friend who their relationship&nbsp;
is not that healthy, and they've said that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.840 --> 01:09:12.720
at any given time, one of them wants to leave,&nbsp;
but there's never been a time when both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.720 --> 01:09:16.560
them want to leave, and that's why they've stuck&nbsp;
together. And so I think, if there's one person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:20.440
in the relationship that is willing to compromise&nbsp;
and make it work, there's a good chance that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.440 --> 01:09:25.920
going to work over time. I don't think I've&nbsp;
ever said this, I really have three major&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:31.494
life goals. I want to stay married, I don't want&nbsp;
to get fat, and I want to be there for my kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:31.494 --> 01:09:32.720
and I think those are just pithy ways to be like —
Tim Ferriss: Is that in weighted order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:32.720 --> 01:09:35.387
one to three?
Morgan Housel: Probably not, probably not.&nbsp;

01:09:35.387 --> 01:09:37.560
Tim Ferriss: I was kidding.
Morgan Housel: But I think that's respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:37.560 --> 01:09:41.200
for my relationships, respect for my body, and&nbsp;
respect for my family is one other way that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.200 --> 01:09:45.600
could put that. And I think those are really the&nbsp;
goals that, if I'm on my death bed and I have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:45.600 --> 01:09:50.920
achieved those, I'm going to have some regrets.&nbsp;
If those are that powerful, then you're willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:50.920 --> 01:09:55.680
compromise on a billion different things to make&nbsp;
it work. And there's a friend of mine named Brent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.680 --> 01:10:01.000
Beshore who says, "If both you and your spouse&nbsp;
want to serve each other and expect nothing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.000 --> 01:10:05.800
return, then it works. I want to serve my wife and&nbsp;
I don't expect her to serve me. And, if both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:05.800 --> 01:10:09.760
you do that, you're always going to be pleasantly&nbsp;
surprised. You're always going to be like, 'Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:09.760 --> 01:10:13.560
you did this for me.' You're always going to be&nbsp;
in the sense of awe of the other person." That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:13.560 --> 01:10:18.800
is idealized, of course. It's much easier to&nbsp;
say than to do, but I think it's a pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:18.800 --> 01:10:23.640
guiding philosophy once you be like, "I'm in the&nbsp;
service industry, I want to try to serve my wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:24.280 --> 01:10:28.840
and I don't want to expect much in return." If I&nbsp;
can do those things, I'm probably going to wake up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:28.840 --> 01:10:32.800
pretty satisfied with the relationship most days.
Tim Ferriss: That's probably true for life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.800 --> 01:10:38.760
broadly speaking, in the sense that reality&nbsp;
minus expectations equals state of being.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.960 --> 01:10:49.800
Let me ask you a question that I brought up a&nbsp;
little bit earlier. I've never heard it posed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:54.760
or read it posed, and so I'm curious to know how&nbsp;
you might answer this if you had a gun against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.760 --> 01:11:01.120
head, because I'm not saying this immediately&nbsp;
produces answers, but if you had to answer it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:04.440
or answer it for other people, what&nbsp;
hassle are you trying to eliminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.440 --> 01:11:09.320
that's actually an unavoidable cost of success?
And the reason that I want to return to this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:09.320 --> 01:11:19.320
that, and this ties into the question of money,&nbsp;
happiness, contentment, et cetera, is that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:26.920
people who achieve what would be considered by&nbsp;
most financial success are people who have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.920 --> 01:11:32.160
rewarded for solving problems over long periods&nbsp;
of time, and they probably identify as being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:32.160 --> 01:11:37.960
good problem-solver. And they are therefore&nbsp;
conditioned, their behaviors are shaped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:45.840
to look for problems to solve, and that does&nbsp;
not always foster a sense of well-being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:45.840 --> 01:11:50.720
if that makes sense. Yeah. What hassle are&nbsp;
you trying to eliminate that's actually an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:50.720 --> 01:11:55.240
unavoidable cost of success? How would you answer&nbsp;
this, or how have you answered it, or how have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.240 --> 01:11:59.000
you seen other people come up with answers?
Morgan Housel: I'll give you a technical answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:02.920
and then maybe a more personal answer.&nbsp;
I think, when I wrote that question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.920 --> 01:12:06.000
I was probably thinking of stock&nbsp;
market volatility, very technical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:10.120
probably not where you thought I was going. A&nbsp;
lot of investors try to eliminate volatility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:10.120 --> 01:12:13.320
and they view it as a burden and something&nbsp;
that you should get rid of when, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:13.320 --> 01:12:17.280
it's like, no, that's the cost of success.
Tim Ferriss: It's the fee and not the penalty.&nbsp;

01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:20.600
Morgan Housel: It's the fee and not the penalty.&nbsp;
That's probably what I was thinking. One thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.600 --> 01:12:26.907
that comes to mind is a blog post that you wrote&nbsp;
many years ago, I think, about the cost of fame.&nbsp;

01:12:26.907 --> 01:12:29.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, "11&nbsp;
Reasons Not to Become Famous."&nbsp;

01:12:29.520 --> 01:12:33.520
Morgan Housel: Yes, and you were very open and&nbsp;
forthcoming about what it's done to your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.520 --> 01:12:41.800
life. And anyone who reads that will read that&nbsp;
and say some version of, "I don't envy what Tim's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.800 --> 01:12:46.800
going through." And the irony of coming to that&nbsp;
conclusion after millions of people will look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:46.800 --> 01:12:50.480
the books that you've sold and the money that&nbsp;
you've made and the downloads of your podcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.480 --> 01:12:54.800
would say, "I want to be like Tim," and then you&nbsp;
read that and it's like, "I don't know. Maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.800 --> 01:13:00.240
but I don't know." It's not at all clear anymore.
Among the top 10 richest people in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:00.240 --> 01:13:06.480
there are, I think, 14 cumulative divorces,&nbsp;
which is obviously a massive outlier within&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.480 --> 01:13:12.080
there. And I think the reason why that is is&nbsp;
because to become very successful, you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:12.080 --> 01:13:17.600
devote every waking hour to your career. And&nbsp;
so your spouse, your children, your friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.600 --> 01:13:22.040
"Bye. Sorry, don't have any time for you."
If you read the biography, The Snowball, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.040 --> 01:13:26.320
I reread recently, it's a biography of Warren&nbsp;
Buffett, as someone who's been such a Buffett&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.320 --> 01:13:30.960
fanboy my entire life, and a lot of people will&nbsp;
fall into that category, you read his biography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:36.880
and at the end, you're like, "To tell you the&nbsp;
truth, not the life that I want." He has devoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.880 --> 01:13:42.160
every waking hour since he's been 11 years old&nbsp;
to picking stocks, that's not an exaggeration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.160 --> 01:13:47.280
and it came at the expense of his personal life,&nbsp;
his marriage, his children. There was an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:47.280 --> 01:13:51.320
with Elon Musk several years ago where they're&nbsp;
talking about how hard he works, and he breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.320 --> 01:13:56.640
down in tears and he says, "All of this has come&nbsp;
at the expense of spending time with my children."&nbsp;

01:13:56.640 --> 01:14:01.520
And it's like I don't want that. There's not&nbsp;
any version of life in which I would be on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:01.520 --> 01:14:04.800
death bed and say, "That was worth it. I made a&nbsp;
ton of money, but who are these children that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.800 --> 01:14:12.640
had? I don't even know them." And so I think, if&nbsp;
you want the success, but you want to eliminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:17.360
the downsides of it, you're completely fooling&nbsp;
yourself. You have to make a decision. I would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.360 --> 01:14:21.640
willing to bet too, although I don't want to put&nbsp;
words in your mouth, that despite that blog post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:21.640 --> 01:14:24.840
when you're talking about all of the&nbsp;
downsides that you have experienced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.840 --> 01:14:30.600
if you had to go back to before, when you wrote&nbsp;
The 4-Hour Workweek, you would not change your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:30.600 --> 01:14:35.200
mind. You would still do it. The cost was&nbsp;
worth it. I don't want to put words into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:35.200 --> 01:14:41.200
your mouth, but I'm willing to bet that's true.
Tim Ferriss: I would say it's a both/and. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:45.960
are ways in which it is worth it, and there are&nbsp;
many ways in which it is not worth it, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:45.960 --> 01:14:51.760
what makes it so tricky on a forward-looking&nbsp;
basis, thinking about decisions. Although it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.760 --> 01:15:05.320
easier for me now because I've realized some of&nbsp;
the Faustian bargains, and I've also realized how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:05.320 --> 01:15:11.960
to your earlier point, how much money is an&nbsp;
amplifier rather than a savior, if that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:11.960 --> 01:15:15.760
sense. And, of course, it depends on where you are&nbsp;
on a Maslow's hierarchy of needs, but chances are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.760 --> 01:15:21.920
if you're listening to this podcast and you've&nbsp;
made it however long, hour, hour and a half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.920 --> 01:15:30.520
that using the global average as a reference&nbsp;
point, you are in the top one percent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.520 --> 01:15:39.720
you are doing very well from a global standpoint.
I'd say a few things just to give myself a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:39.720 --> 01:15:46.800
bit of slack, the younger self, that I don't&nbsp;
think hope is trivial, and that seems like such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:46.800 --> 01:15:55.320
a self-evident statement. And I'm not sure if&nbsp;
we talked about this, but there are some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.320 --> 01:16:01.280
I've come across who are like, "Oh, lotteries.&nbsp;
Lotteries are taxes on the dumb," and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.280 --> 01:16:09.520
about it, I'm like, "Well, if you feel trapped,&nbsp;
if you don't have mobility, and 10 bucks, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.520 --> 01:16:17.200
the Powerball gets over $500 million, buys you a&nbsp;
week or a couple of days of hope and excitement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:17.200 --> 01:16:19.320
is that really a bad investment?"
Morgan Housel: Totally worth it.&nbsp;

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:22.640
Tim Ferriss: I would actually push back and say&nbsp;
it's actually probably not a bad investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.640 --> 01:16:26.920
it's actually probably a great investment, as&nbsp;
long as you don't overdo it. And, similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:26.920 --> 01:16:33.920
I think that, without the delusion early on&nbsp;
that money would fix all of these problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:40.920
it would resolve all of this inner conflict&nbsp;
and turmoil and insecurity — I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:40.920 --> 01:16:46.520
was actually a very valuable delusion.
What was my alternative? Compared to what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:46.520 --> 01:16:50.760
I think, is a question we need to ask more often.&nbsp;
Risky, compared to what? Is it this, compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:50.760 --> 01:16:54.480
what? Is that person a good match, compared&nbsp;
to what? And, for me, it was like, all right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:54.480 --> 01:17:02.920
what was the best alternative, to just not have&nbsp;
any hope, to assume that this steady state of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:02.920 --> 01:17:07.560
at the time, quite a bit of suffering and&nbsp;
depression was going to last forever? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:07.560 --> 01:17:12.640
don't think that was a viable alternative.
Even though I've arrived at a point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:20.240
I need to find other solutions, I think that's&nbsp;
better than having given up hope 20, 30 years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:20.240 --> 01:17:28.240
But I would've made some very different tactical&nbsp;
decisions along the way to minimize some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.240 --> 01:17:33.320
risks and downsides for sure, but it's tricky.
Morgan Housel: Even if that's, in hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.320 --> 01:17:37.080
definitely true, I think it would've been&nbsp;
impossible because there's no way for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:37.080 --> 01:17:42.000
to learn about the downsides of fame other than&nbsp;
to go through them. It almost would've been very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:42.000 --> 01:17:45.280
difficult to preempt it. I think there are some&nbsp;
things to do. Back to the $8 billion family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.280 --> 01:17:50.280
they learned vicariously through other families&nbsp;
what not to do, and they preempted a lot of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.280 --> 01:17:54.600
so to the extent that you can learn those mistakes&nbsp;
from other people — and I would say reading that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.600 --> 01:18:01.920
blog post from you, Tim, had a profound impact on&nbsp;
me. And I think what a lot of people who are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:01.920 --> 01:18:07.200
any degree of public spotlight don't understand&nbsp;
is that you think you're gaining attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:07.200 --> 01:18:10.360
but what you're actually fostering is&nbsp;
envy, and envy will come back to haunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.360 --> 01:18:17.240
you tenfold. And so be very careful when you&nbsp;
put your successes out there, because even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.240 --> 01:18:21.360
for people who are clapping for you, they're&nbsp;
clapping for you while they're biting their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:21.360 --> 01:18:25.080
lip because they're envious of what you have.
Tim Ferriss: And even if they don't feel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.080 --> 01:18:30.400
envy in the moment, and I may have mentioned&nbsp;
this in that piece, but it's the poles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:30.400 --> 01:18:35.600
the extremes switch sides very easily. And&nbsp;
although it tends to be unidirectional in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:35.600 --> 01:18:47.760
the sense that the people who are your biggest,&nbsp;
most extreme fans, have the most dedication, are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:47.760 --> 01:18:52.560
in my experience, this is not my own experience,&nbsp;
I've spoken to a lot of people who would reinforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:52.560 --> 01:19:00.840
this, they can switch to being the most&nbsp;
violent, terrifying attackers very easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:00.840 --> 01:19:07.120
and that can get physically dangerous. There are&nbsp;
many examples of that. I don't have to get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:07.120 --> 01:19:14.160
it. But that particular set of experiences has&nbsp;
also led me to rethink, honestly, the podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.160 --> 01:19:19.920
in the sense that to compete — and I'm less and&nbsp;
less interested in competing in most things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:24.520
although, by nature, I'm very competitive. I enjoy&nbsp;
competing. I enjoy winning, I guess, is a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:24.520 --> 01:19:29.120
way to put it, or maybe I just hate losing. Who&nbsp;
knows? We can psychoanalyze that another time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.520 --> 01:19:39.280
to compete right now, you need to, as a podcaster&nbsp;
in this type of format, not true crime, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.280 --> 01:19:45.160
exceptions like This American Life, but if you are&nbsp;
interview format, if you really want to compete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.160 --> 01:19:53.240
you basically have to produce a television show.
And one, I think, of the mostly hidden risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.240 --> 01:19:58.000
which is obvious, I think, in retrospect, when&nbsp;
people look back a year or five years from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:58.000 --> 01:20:02.760
it will seem very obvious and they'll say, "Well,&nbsp;
how could it have turned out any different?" But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:02.760 --> 01:20:13.080
the risks of video microfame, or macrofame, and&nbsp;
the degree to which a much higher percentage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:13.080 --> 01:20:17.720
the population is going to be subject to&nbsp;
stalkers and death threats and very, very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.720 --> 01:20:22.120
very scary things, people showing up at their&nbsp;
homes because they bought it in their own name&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:22.120 --> 01:20:31.160
and not through an LLC or trust or whatever. That,&nbsp;
I think, is going to be a cost that a lot of folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.160 --> 01:20:37.560
are not seeing down the pike that I think is&nbsp;
unavoidable. For me, personally, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:45.120
as I shift into new chapters in my life, I've been&nbsp;
thinking about, meditating on, writing on, quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:55.600
extensively how I want to adapt or stop or modify&nbsp;
what I'm doing to mitigate some of those risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:55.600 --> 01:21:03.560
because I do view them as unavoidable. And I just&nbsp;
don't think, most people having never experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.560 --> 01:21:06.960
that, having never been in a car crash, they're&nbsp;
like, "What do you mean a car crash?" Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:06.960 --> 01:21:11.040
"Sure, I'll wear my seatbelt, but I don't have&nbsp;
to pay attention to A, B, and C, and D factors."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:11.040 --> 01:21:16.120
It's like, "No, you probably should because the&nbsp;
downside risk is so high." Anyway, not to paint&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:16.120 --> 01:21:21.320
a dark picture. I think a lot of these things can&nbsp;
be avoided, but the temptation, like you said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:21.320 --> 01:21:26.240
and the performative aspect of so much of this&nbsp;
on say social media, and the reinforcement and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.240 --> 01:21:34.520
the way that these platforms have been designed&nbsp;
to take advantage of the baser drives and fears&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:34.520 --> 01:21:39.440
that humans have is remarkable. They're very&nbsp;
well-tuned. Maybe it's easy for me to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:39.440 --> 01:21:45.000
right, because I've had enough success on&nbsp;
say, the podcast that I could afford to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:45.000 --> 01:21:48.680
the cord if I wanted and do something else.
Morgan Housel: Right. And see that's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.680 --> 01:21:55.800
interesting to me are the young people on social&nbsp;
media who are very reasonably waving their arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:55.800 --> 01:21:58.960
to get attention because they don't have any&nbsp;
attention yet, and they're trying to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:58.960 --> 01:22:02.480
through. I see this with a lot of new people on&nbsp;
Twitter, a lot of new bloggers sometimes, new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:02.480 --> 01:22:07.520
podcasters. How do you stand out? You stand out&nbsp;
by being ridiculous. A lot of new politicians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.520 --> 01:22:10.720
by the way, some of whom are running for office&nbsp;
right now. If you're not a household name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:10.720 --> 01:22:15.400
how do you become one by saying the most&nbsp;
ridiculous thing possible? So that's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:15.400 --> 01:22:21.280
when attention is the currency and the only way&nbsp;
to become wealthy in that sense is to do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:21.280 --> 01:22:26.960
so far out of the norm and norm breaking.
It's a really dangerous world to live in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:27.480 --> 01:22:36.480
because social media came to be when I was in&nbsp;
my early twenties, but I see my son who's seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.480 --> 01:22:41.600
and is already interacting with YouTube, not any&nbsp;
other social media platforms, but even in YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:41.600 --> 01:22:46.320
the influence that it has on him is astounding.&nbsp;
And growing up with MrBeast, which I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

