that would've been so far outside of any&nbsp;
mental schema for thinking about anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:50.600 --> 01:37:55.800
that I would do. It wouldn't have occurred to me.
Morgan Housel: Tim, when you think about the arc&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:55.800 --> 01:38:00.360
of your career, would you put the podcast above&nbsp;
The 4-Hour Workweek in terms of importance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:00.360 --> 01:38:05.320
success, however you want to measure that?
Tim Ferriss: I don't think I would because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.320 --> 01:38:10.720
if I separate them out into discreet independent&nbsp;
pieces, I think that's a mistake since there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:10.720 --> 01:38:14.600
a domino effect. And without that first domino,&nbsp;
the second and the third and the fourth domino&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:14.600 --> 01:38:22.120
don't tip over. So I would have to still give The&nbsp;
4-Hour Workweek by far, love it or hate it for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:22.120 --> 01:38:30.760
blessing and the curse, that is what made&nbsp;
everything subsequently possible. So I still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:30.760 --> 01:38:38.680
to give The 4-Hour Workweek the vast majority of&nbsp;
credit. Remarkably, that book and probably Tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:38.680 --> 01:38:46.960
of Titans are my two bestselling books still to&nbsp;
this day. And I would say a third, probably almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:46.960 --> 01:38:54.840
all of the tools referenced in The 4-Hour Workweek&nbsp;
are out of date. And that makes me very happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:54.840 --> 01:39:05.560
though, because the philosophical backbone, the&nbsp;
core fundamental pieces are timeless in a sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:05.560 --> 01:39:09.400
They really don't have an expiration date.
So I've been very pleased to see that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:09.400 --> 01:39:14.000
but it's nonetheless remarkable that the book&nbsp;
continues to be read and recommended despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:14.000 --> 01:39:20.240
fact that I'm telling people to go to a magazine&nbsp;
rack and use various tools to determine readership&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:20.240 --> 01:39:28.120
of magazines as a way of sizing audience. I&nbsp;
mean, that's Model T or maybe the horse-drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:28.120 --> 01:39:31.920
carriage compared to what we can do today.&nbsp;
So at some point, perhaps I'll update that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:31.920 --> 01:39:36.400
but that's a losing game. Also, updating tools&nbsp;
is just a losing game. I could have updated three&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:36.400 --> 01:39:43.040
years ago and then ChatGPT and other LLMs come&nbsp;
along and I have to do a complete revision after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:43.040 --> 01:39:48.920
that. So I assume the principles will enable&nbsp;
people to find the tools. But the podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:48.920 --> 01:39:57.200
I would say certainly at this point, nine out of&nbsp;
10 people who come up to me on the street will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:57.200 --> 01:40:01.787
mention the podcast and will not mention any book.
Morgan Housel: Yeah, that's astounding.&nbsp;

01:40:01.787 --> 01:40:08.800
Tim Ferriss: Which is astounding. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
I do think the decay rate on the podcast is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:08.800 --> 01:40:18.440
higher and will be on a totality much faster than&nbsp;
the book. In other words, not only will people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:18.440 --> 01:40:24.160
forget a given episode, they will forget that I&nbsp;
had a podcast or anything related to my podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.160 --> 01:40:30.840
I think much faster than they will the books&nbsp;
simply because there is so much fucking noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:30.840 --> 01:40:37.520
there's so much. The replacement rate is so high&nbsp;
and people can only hold so much in their heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.520 --> 01:40:49.160
self included. So the sort of first in first out&nbsp;
sort of mental archiving is such that I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:49.160 --> 01:40:53.360
if I stopped podcasting today and people continued&nbsp;
listening to podcasts who were devout listeners of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:53.360 --> 01:40:59.640
The Tim Ferriss Show, I think they would forget&nbsp;
about The Tim Ferriss Show within a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.640 --> 01:41:05.600
one or two years if I'm being optimistic. Whereas&nbsp;
I do think the books, for whatever reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:05.600 --> 01:41:16.063
seem to have a more persistent, enduring&nbsp;
foothold in the mind than in the podcast.&nbsp;

01:41:16.063 --> 01:41:17.680
Morgan Housel: I wonder if —
Tim Ferriss: Even if it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:17.680 --> 01:41:21.680
best episode I've ever put out. Doesn't matter.
Morgan Housel: I don't disagree with it. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:21.680 --> 01:41:25.400
just trying to figure out why that would&nbsp;
be. And maybe it's because in a book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.400 --> 01:41:31.560
if it's 20 chapters, that's all you got. It's&nbsp;
just 20 chapters in front of you. So it gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:31.560 --> 01:41:36.880
the impression or the truth that what is there is&nbsp;
profound. Whereas if you open up Apple Podcasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:36.880 --> 01:41:41.120
there are three million podcasts or whatever there&nbsp;
is, and each of those has dozens or hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:41.120 --> 01:41:46.240
episodes. It's so much in there that it's hard&nbsp;
to take any single bit of it seriously. But maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.240 --> 01:41:49.920
that's true for books too. You walk into Barnes &amp;&nbsp;
Noble, there's a million books in there as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:49.920 --> 01:41:53.520
But when you're holding a book, it's like, "This&nbsp;
is all that's in front of me right now, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:53.520 --> 01:41:58.000
I need to pay attention to every word." Whereas&nbsp;
a podcast, it's just a living, breathing thing.&nbsp;

01:41:58.000 --> 01:42:04.800
And if you watch the daily news, particularly&nbsp;
24/7 cable news, none of it should be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:04.800 --> 01:42:09.200
profound because there's just so much of it.&nbsp;
If you're just constantly updating people on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.200 --> 01:42:13.480
the news of the hour, odds are it's not that&nbsp;
important. Whereas if there was a one-hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.480 --> 01:42:18.640
newscast per year that was updating you on the&nbsp;
most important news of the previous 12 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:18.640 --> 01:42:22.600
it would be very important. You have to watch&nbsp;
that. It's very, very important. So I think a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:22.600 --> 01:42:26.440
lot of it is just the scale, that it's much&nbsp;
easier to make a podcast episode than it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:26.440 --> 01:42:30.600
to publish a book chapter in physical form.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I agree with that. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:30.600 --> 01:42:40.040
it's spitballing ideas. I also think that if&nbsp;
someone commits to reading a book, if they finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:40.040 --> 01:42:47.200
it, especially if the assumption is buying a&nbsp;
nonfiction book that is prescriptive in its advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:47.200 --> 01:42:52.560
is that you are going to take action after a book.&nbsp;
There are a few things that happen in the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:52.560 --> 01:42:59.760
of doing that. Number one, you're contextualizing&nbsp;
the book for your own life as you go. Number two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:59.760 --> 01:43:05.160
you're harnessing sunk cost fallacy in a sense,&nbsp;
because you've put more time into this book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:05.160 --> 01:43:10.468
I think you are more likely to try to take&nbsp;
some type of action to justify that investment.&nbsp;

01:43:10.468 --> 01:43:15.160
And I would say also with the book, and&nbsp;
single tasking, you have captive attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:15.160 --> 01:43:20.880
and I think people are more engaged. This is&nbsp;
true for a novel versus say, a film also in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:20.880 --> 01:43:29.520
co-creating the visual experience of traveling&nbsp;
through these pages. And then when people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:29.520 --> 01:43:35.640
if they take action after reading a book, that&nbsp;
is an extension of the experience of reading the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:35.640 --> 01:43:41.480
book and is forever associated with that book,&nbsp;
as are I think downstream effects of those first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.480 --> 01:43:49.120
steps. Whereas listening to a podcast, I think the&nbsp;
preset mentality coming into it, and this is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.120 --> 01:43:54.520
for me too when I'm listening to a podcast, is I'm&nbsp;
listening to something while I'm going for a walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:54.520 --> 01:44:02.560
while doing something else, and I am absorbing&nbsp;
information. But the expectation is not given&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.560 --> 01:44:07.480
the absurd volume of podcasts out there, and also&nbsp;
just the absurd volume of podcasts and audiobooks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.480 --> 01:44:15.160
that I listen to, the assumption I think in my&nbsp;
mind is passive ingestion less than I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:15.160 --> 01:44:19.960
to take some notes and actually commit to making&nbsp;
meaningful change in my life after I listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.960 --> 01:44:22.520
this podcast. I wish it were different.
Morgan Housel: Yeah, I agree. No, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:22.520 --> 01:44:28.000
definitely true. When I do listen to podcasts,&nbsp;
it's pleasure. And I think when I read books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:28.000 --> 01:44:31.000
I'm like, "I really want to learn something&nbsp;
here and take notes and highlight it and read&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:31.000 --> 01:44:34.960
wise and really get something out of this."&nbsp;
What I did start doing recently that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.960 --> 01:44:39.640
very helpful is when I'm listening to a podcast&nbsp;
and I hear something that's really profound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:39.640 --> 01:44:44.600
I'll just screenshot it and therefore I have it&nbsp;
timestamped of like, "Oh, on Tim's podcast at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:44.600 --> 01:44:50.240
one minute and 23 seconds," and then I'll go back&nbsp;
and find the transcript and try to take notes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:50.240 --> 01:44:54.320
way. That's been the best note-taking process that&nbsp;
I've had for podcasts to try to gain more insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:54.320 --> 01:45:01.440
out of it rather than just passive pleasure.
Tim Ferriss: Speaking of books, so let's go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:01.440 --> 01:45:07.360
through a list, and I'd love for you to pick one&nbsp;
or two. We can certainly talk about all of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:07.360 --> 01:45:13.160
but great books that you've read&nbsp;
lately, Triangle Fire, Empty Mansions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:13.160 --> 01:45:20.000
The Strange Last Voyage of Donald Crowhurst, and&nbsp;
then one that is a little cryptic, Robert Caro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:20.000 --> 01:45:24.400
if I'm getting the pronunciation right,&nbsp;
Working. I don't know what that refers to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:24.400 --> 01:45:30.920
Maybe we start there because I believe that is&nbsp;
the author of The Power Broker. Could you maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:30.920 --> 01:45:35.760
start with Robert and what that refers to?
Morgan Housel: Robert Caro is probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:35.760 --> 01:45:39.800
greatest biographer of all time. He's certainly&nbsp;
the most thorough biographer of all time. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:39.800 --> 01:45:45.480
of his books take between 10 and 30 years for him&nbsp;
to write. He's done biographies on Lyndon Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:45.480 --> 01:45:50.480
and Robert Moses, who was an instrumental&nbsp;
figure in building the modern New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:50.480 --> 01:45:55.080
and he's just ridiculously thorough.&nbsp;
And so he wrote a book called Working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:55.080 --> 01:46:01.680
which is his life and practice and strategy as a&nbsp;
writer, how did he become a writer and how does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.680 --> 01:46:08.840
he do it? And you realize that his talent is just&nbsp;
patience. He will sit in an archive for 15 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.840 --> 01:46:14.200
And he has this motto, "Turn Every Page." So he&nbsp;
talks about when he decided to do a biography&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:14.200 --> 01:46:20.480
of Lyndon Johnson. The first thing that he did is&nbsp;
he moved his wife to the middle of nowhere Texas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:20.480 --> 01:46:23.920
where Lyndon Johnson grew up. And he said,&nbsp;
"There's no way that he could understand the man&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:23.920 --> 01:46:29.225
unless he lived in the town that he grew up in."
Tim Ferriss: Full understanding wife.&nbsp;

01:46:29.225 --> 01:46:32.040
Morgan Housel: Exactly. There's a funny quote&nbsp;
where his wife said, "Why couldn't you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:32.040 --> 01:46:36.560
done a biography of Napoleon?" And they could&nbsp;
have moved to Paris or something. So he moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:36.560 --> 01:46:41.200
there, and then he sits and he goes to the Lyndon&nbsp;
Johnson Presidential Library, and I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:41.200 --> 01:46:47.520
literally like 8,000 boxes of material. And he&nbsp;
goes through every page. He goes through every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:47.520 --> 01:46:52.104
page. It takes him decades to do this. And so&nbsp;
there's just no one that is as thorough as —&nbsp;

01:46:52.104 --> 01:46:55.520
Tim Ferriss: It sounds like a prison sentence.
Morgan Housel: Seriously. But I think he loves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:55.520 --> 01:47:00.240
every second of it. I think the hunt for him is&nbsp;
incredible. So even with something like Lyndon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.240 --> 01:47:04.120
Johnson, Robert Moses is a little more obscure,&nbsp;
but everybody knows Lyndon Johnson. His life&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:04.120 --> 01:47:08.680
was so well documented. But Robert Caro will&nbsp;
find these nuggets that no one else has ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.680 --> 01:47:13.840
uncovered before, and I have so much admiration&nbsp;
for that in terms of sticking out as a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:13.840 --> 01:47:18.120
and doing something different. A crazy stat&nbsp;
and you can take this several different ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.120 --> 01:47:24.160
but there are 1,100 biographies of Winston&nbsp;
Churchill, published biographies of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:24.160 --> 01:47:29.040
person talking about the same life. But I bet&nbsp;
there's like 50 of those that really stand out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:29.040 --> 01:47:33.560
because they found a different angle. They found&nbsp;
a different fact, or they told a better story.&nbsp;

01:47:35.040 --> 01:47:40.480
Here's how I'd phrase this. Ken Burns, who does&nbsp;
the documentaries, his skill is telling a story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:40.480 --> 01:47:44.680
and most of the information in his documentaries&nbsp;
is already well-known about the Civil War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:44.680 --> 01:47:48.040
World War II, whatever it would be. It's&nbsp;
not new information, but he tells such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.040 --> 01:47:53.760
a good story that it's captivating and&nbsp;
you're going to watch it. Robert Caro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.760 --> 01:47:59.280
and I mean this with the most respect, is not the&nbsp;
best storyteller, but his ability to uncover new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:59.280 --> 01:48:05.320
information is completely unparalleled to everyone&nbsp;
else. So I admire him that his skill is just,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:05.320 --> 01:48:10.147
he works harder and he's more patient&nbsp;
than any other writer that's ever existed.&nbsp;

01:48:10.147 --> 01:48:16.160
Tim Ferriss: Is there anything else that you took&nbsp;
from that book that you have applied or hope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:16.160 --> 01:48:20.240
apply to your own writing or creative process?
Morgan Housel: One of it that I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:20.920 --> 01:48:25.240
and I'm not saying this to blow smoke, but it&nbsp;
reminds me of you, is that in his interviewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:25.240 --> 01:48:30.880
process when he's interviewing people, he would&nbsp;
always just stop and say, "Tell me more. Tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.880 --> 01:48:37.120
me more. Keep going, tell me more." There was&nbsp;
one scene from the book where he's interviewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:37.120 --> 01:48:42.720
Lyndon Johnson's chauffeur who was with him&nbsp;
for his entire career as a politician. Lyndon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.720 --> 01:48:48.320
Johnson did the same driver. And so the driver&nbsp;
saw everything. And Robert Caro says, "Tell me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.320 --> 01:48:53.040
when Lyndon Johnson was in the backseat of the&nbsp;
car when he was campaigning, what was he doing?"&nbsp;

01:48:53.040 --> 01:48:56.800
And the chauffeur's like, "I don't know. I wasn't&nbsp;
paying attention." But he just kept asking, "No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:56.800 --> 01:49:00.360
no, tell me more. What was he doing? What was&nbsp;
he talking about? What'd he do?" And he kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.360 --> 01:49:04.120
getting the answer, "I don't know. I wasn't&nbsp;
paying attention." And finally, after weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:04.120 --> 01:49:08.240
of asking the same question, the chauffeur&nbsp;
says, "You know what? After campaign rallies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.240 --> 01:49:13.200
Lyndon Johnson would talk to himself in the&nbsp;
backseat of the car, and he would say to himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:13.200 --> 01:49:17.920
mumbling to himself, 'This worked, this didn't,&nbsp;
I need to get better at this. I need to double&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:17.920 --> 01:49:22.720
down on this.'" That's fascinating that he would&nbsp;
talk to him himself. And it took Robert Caro weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:22.720 --> 01:49:25.960
of asking the same question before.
Tim Ferriss: It's like a black side&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:25.960 --> 01:49:29.600
interrogation just wearing him down.
Morgan Housel: Yes. But I think that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:29.600 --> 01:49:34.480
true for everyone, and I think a lot of it is,&nbsp;
if you asked me what did I do last week? I'd say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:34.480 --> 01:49:38.840
"Honestly, I don't really remember." But if you&nbsp;
kept asking, I probably did something that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.840 --> 01:49:43.200
meaningful to me, if you kept asking and kept&nbsp;
pushing, then, the memories start to bubble up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:43.200 --> 01:49:48.080
And I think Robert Caro was just a pro at that.
Tim Ferriss: Hm. What if these other books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.080 --> 01:49:53.240
maybe we pick one, if you had to pick two from&nbsp;
this list, so Triangle Fire, Empty Mansions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:53.240 --> 01:49:58.360
The Strange Last Voyage of Donald Crowhurst.&nbsp;
Or maybe you can give just a very brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.360 --> 01:50:03.720
not to leave people hanging, what are these&nbsp;
books? And then, if you had to pick one more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.720 --> 01:50:08.265
if you could only pick two, for you personally&nbsp;
from this list, what the other one would be?&nbsp;

01:50:08.265 --> 01:50:11.200
Morgan Housel: Yeah. Here's what's really&nbsp;
interesting about the Triangle Fire. I think most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.200 --> 01:50:14.480
if not everyone here listening to&nbsp;
this knows what the Triangle Fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:14.480 --> 01:50:15.985
is. It was a fire in the early 1900s —
Tim Ferriss: I have no idea.&nbsp;

01:50:15.985 --> 01:50:21.240
Morgan Housel: Oh, okay, this is great, then. It&nbsp;
was a fire and the Triangle Shirtwaist Company in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:21.240 --> 01:50:24.800
New York City, which was a company in,&nbsp;
I think it was 1911, that was making —&nbsp;

01:50:24.800 --> 01:50:27.560
Tim Ferriss: Everybody knows about&nbsp;
this. I feel like an idiot. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.560 --> 01:50:30.640
never heard anything about this. All right.
Morgan Housel: Okay. No, this is even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:30.640 --> 01:50:36.600
then. It was a fire that took place in this&nbsp;
shirt garment factory in New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:36.600 --> 01:50:40.480
and it was in the middle of the workday&nbsp;
and there were several hundred, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:40.480 --> 01:50:45.760
virtually all young women who were making shirts&nbsp;
in New York City in 1911 and a fire took place on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:45.760 --> 01:50:52.640
the factory. The punchline of the story was very&nbsp;
tragic. I think 200 of them died, A lot of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:52.640 --> 01:50:57.000
jumped out of the ninth storey of the building.
The building is now, I think it's either part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.000 --> 01:51:01.200
or right next to NYU. The building still exists,&nbsp;
and there's a plaque in front of the building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:01.200 --> 01:51:05.560
but it was horrendous. Several hundred of them&nbsp;
burned to death. Several of them, several dozen of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:05.560 --> 01:51:10.560
them jumped out of the building. It was absolutely&nbsp;
horrendous. And I think, until 9/11, it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:10.560 --> 01:51:15.760
deadliest fire in New York City, the deadliest&nbsp;
burning fire. It was just an utter tragedy.&nbsp;

01:51:15.760 --> 01:51:19.440
And when I saw a book about this — I had&nbsp;
heard about this in documentaries and whatnot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:19.440 --> 01:51:24.600
but I thought, how can you make a 250-page book&nbsp;
about a fire that, from start to finish was 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:24.600 --> 01:51:29.200
minutes? How do you make a book? There's got to&nbsp;
be so much rambling in here. And there wasn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:29.200 --> 01:51:34.120
because actually, the actual fire was just&nbsp;
a seed of a much bigger story, which was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:34.120 --> 01:51:38.560
after the Triangle Shirtwaist Fire, what happened&nbsp;
is all of the fire escapes in the building were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.560 --> 01:51:44.400
locked intentionally so that the workers, these&nbsp;
women, could not take an unscheduled break. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:44.400 --> 01:51:47.345
literally lock you in a room to work.
Tim Ferriss: Ugh. That's so bad.&nbsp;

01:51:47.345 --> 01:51:50.280
Morgan Housel: So when there was a fire, they all&nbsp;
went to the fire escapes and they were locked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.280 --> 01:51:53.880
and those women either burned to&nbsp;
death or jumped out of the building.&nbsp;

01:51:53.880 --> 01:52:00.520
This set off this unbelievable moral panic&nbsp;
across the United States, of worker rights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:00.520 --> 01:52:05.600
And one of the witnesses of the fire, who&nbsp;
stood on the sidewalk and watched the women&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:05.600 --> 01:52:10.520
jump out of the building, was a young woman&nbsp;
named Frances Perkins. And Frances Perkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:10.520 --> 01:52:15.360
a lot of people will know that name, she became&nbsp;
FDR's Secretary of Labor. She was the first female&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.360 --> 01:52:20.880
cabinet member of any administration. And she&nbsp;
was so moved by the Triangle Fire, as were so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:20.880 --> 01:52:25.400
many other people that, after she witnessed it,&nbsp;
she said, "I'm going to devote my life to worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:25.400 --> 01:52:31.840
rights." And after a century or more, several&nbsp;
centuries of just horrendous sweatshop conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:31.840 --> 01:52:37.440
the Triangle Fire was the impetus that was&nbsp;
needed to start all kinds of worker rights.&nbsp;

01:52:37.440 --> 01:52:41.360
And there's a quote from Frances Perkins&nbsp;
when she said, "Without the Triangle Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:41.360 --> 01:52:45.880
there would've been no New Deal," the New Deal of&nbsp;
worker rights in the 1930s that moved everything.&nbsp;

01:52:45.880 --> 01:52:49.840
So much progress in the world starts&nbsp;
when something very tragic happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:50.520 --> 01:52:55.160
and starts when things are really bad. The Great&nbsp;
Depression, World War II, the Triangle Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:55.160 --> 01:53:00.720
so much innovation and improvement comes&nbsp;
from those, because there is no motivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:00.720 --> 01:53:06.840
in life more than just shock and necessity.
So the Triangle Fire, as tragic and awful as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:06.840 --> 01:53:11.680
it was, it really was the reason that we&nbsp;
have so many workplace protections. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:11.680 --> 01:53:15.960
within a year or two of the Triangle Fire,&nbsp;
there were all kinds of new laws in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:15.960 --> 01:53:22.080
York City about worker rights and simple things&nbsp;
like fire escapes and whatnot that didn't exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:22.080 --> 01:53:26.520
until that happened. That's why you can take a&nbsp;
30-minute fire and turn it into a 250-page book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.520 --> 01:53:33.600
is because what the Triangle Fire sparked in terms&nbsp;
of worker rights in America was unprecedented.&nbsp;

