02:06:05.240 --> 02:06:10.800
about investing or philosophy or politics,&nbsp;
you want to stay at 30,000 feet. That's as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:10.800 --> 02:06:16.240
good as you're going to get. A lot of this is&nbsp;
just understanding the behaviors that are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:16.240 --> 02:06:20.240
broad and macro rather than fooling yourself&nbsp;
into thinking that you can understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:20.240 --> 02:06:24.640
finer details of what's going on around you.
Tim Ferriss: Who's this book for? If you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:24.640 --> 02:06:30.240
to build on that, what is the audience for&nbsp;
this book? What should they hope to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:30.240 --> 02:06:35.800
away from the book having learned or absorbed?
Morgan Housel: The answer to that will make will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:35.800 --> 02:06:39.920
and did make every publisher shake&nbsp;
their head, because they don't like it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:39.920 --> 02:06:44.400
but it's the truth for all of my writing.&nbsp;
The answer is, that the book is for me. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:44.400 --> 02:06:48.720
wrote it for me. I wrote Psychology of Money&nbsp;
for me. I wrote all my blog posts for me. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:48.720 --> 02:06:53.320
call it selfish writing. An audience of one.
The reason I do that is not because I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:53.320 --> 02:06:56.880
think of myself as self-centered. The reason&nbsp;
I do it is because I think you do your best&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:56.880 --> 02:07:01.240
work if you are being introspective&nbsp;
about yourself. And quote unquote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:01.240 --> 02:07:06.120
"knowing your audience," that slips into pandering&nbsp;
very quickly. And pandering is the worst writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:06.120 --> 02:07:10.360
Not only is it the worst content, it's the worst&nbsp;
writing style, is to pander to your audience.&nbsp;

02:07:10.360 --> 02:07:15.200
So I wrote this book answering questions that I&nbsp;
have about myself, and I wrote it in a style that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:15.200 --> 02:07:19.080
is interesting to me. And I tell stories that I&nbsp;
think are interesting. There's probably a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:19.080 --> 02:07:23.480
death and destruction because, as you can tell,&nbsp;
that's kind of what I gravitate towards. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:23.480 --> 02:07:28.320
in most of the world, I want to think of myself as&nbsp;
a selfless person, except for writing, and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:28.320 --> 02:07:32.800
it's just me and the keyboard and nobody else. And&nbsp;
I think that that's served me well as a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:32.800 --> 02:07:38.240
just to stick with an audience of one, for myself.
Now, I hope and I think that I can take a leap of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:38.240 --> 02:07:42.120
faith that if I have these questions about&nbsp;
myself, other people probably have it about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:42.120 --> 02:07:46.040
themselves. Not everybody, you're never going to&nbsp;
please everybody, but that's the leap of faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:46.040 --> 02:07:49.520
that I took with psychology and money is,&nbsp;
those topics were really interesting to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:49.520 --> 02:07:53.680
and I bet they're interesting to you too.
That's what I say. Now, I hope at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:53.680 --> 02:07:57.760
of both books, Psychology of Money and Same&nbsp;
As Ever, you become more introspective about&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:57.760 --> 02:08:02.240
